  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At halftime in the football match, the chefs head inside to cook lunch for the film crew, while Chef Barinov demands his hundred thousand rubles up front before stepping in front of the camera.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Так, все в ресторан, готовим обед!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Это было несложно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Мы договаривались до десяти, так что после обеда продолжим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Зачем продолжать свой позор?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну что, вы готовы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, а где деньги?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Быстро учится, умный мальчик.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ничего еще не сняли, а уже деньги просит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Пистолет, где его пистолет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Готовы, давайте снимать!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Камера, мотор идет, поехали!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Сцена девять, дубль один, начали!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Так, все в ресторан, готовим обед!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, everyone into the restaurant, we are cooking lunch!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'so' before landing on 'lunch'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Это было несложно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That was not hard.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'this' before landing on 'not hard'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Мы договаривались до десяти, так что после обеда продолжим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We agreed to play to ten, so we will continue after lunch.' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'agreed' covers a group or the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Зачем продолжать свой позор?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why prolong your disgrace?' Denis brings out the contrast between 'why' and 'to continue' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну что, вы готовы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, are you ready?' Dmitry Nagiyev chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'ready' to keep a polite distance.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, а где деньги?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, where is the money?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'wait' and finishing on 'money'. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Быстро учится, умный мальчик.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He learns fast, smart boy.' Dmitry Nagiyev anchors the line on 'quickly', while 'boy' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ничего еще не сняли, а уже деньги просит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have not filmed a thing yet, and he is already asking for money.' The meaning hinges on 'nothing' working together with 'is asking for' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Пистолет, где его пистолет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The pistol, where is his pistol?' Notice how Russian expresses 'pistol' and 'his' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Готовы, давайте снимать!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are ready, let's film!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'ready' before landing on 'to take off'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Камера, мотор идет, поехали!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Camera, rolling, let's go!' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'here we go' covers a group or the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Сцена девять, дубль один, начали!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Scene nine, take one, action!' The meaning hinges on 'stage' working together with 'started' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
